      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 463,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 376,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63613,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 228,
        "headingId": 376,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>There are no distinctive clinical features.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T16:53:21"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 464,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 377,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63614,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 228,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Mixed endometrial carcinomas are rare; mixed endometrioid and serous carcinomas account for about 10% of endometrial carcinomas {26132201}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T16:53:21"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63615,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 228,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T16:53:21"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63616,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 228,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Some mixed endometrial carcinomas represent synchronous, biologically unrelated collision tumours. Others represent divergent differentiation or progression from low-grade endometrioid carcinoma {30550482; 25677978; 26492180}. Usually, the tumour exhibits the molecular features of each component, but there may be some overlapping features and some degree of molecular ambiguity {25677978}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T16:53:21"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63617,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 228,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T16:53:21"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63618,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 228,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>At least two spatially distinct histotypes of endometrial carcinoma must be identified by histology and immunohistochemistry. This diagnostic category should not be used for morphological variations of endometrioid, clear cell, or serous carcinoma or for carcinoma with ambiguous morphology, dedifferentiated carcinoma, or carcinosarcoma. The most commonly encountered admixture is of endometrioid and serous carcinoma. The other distinctive subtype that may be admixed is clear cell carcinoma. Any percentage of high-grade carcinoma that can be confidently demonstrated is sufficient to label the tumour as a mixed endometrial carcinoma, because even a small percentage of serous or clear cell carcinoma may confer an adverse prognosis and outcome {31318970; 20393071}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T16:53:21"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 381,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63619,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 228,
        "headingId": 381,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T16:53:21"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 469,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 382,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63620,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 228,
        "headingId": 382,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T16:53:21"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 472,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 385,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63621,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 228,
        "headingId": 385,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> endometrial carcinoma with two distinct histological types, in which at least one component is either serous or clear cell; exclusion of dedifferentiated carcinoma and carcinosarcoma.</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em> immunohistochemical demonstration of the two distinct carcinoma types.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T16:53:21"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 470,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 383,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63622,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 228,
        "headingId": 383,
        "headingText": "<p>This entity is staged according to the Union for International Cancer Control (UICC) TNM classification (see <em>TNM staging of tumours of the uterus&nbsp;&ndash; endometrium</em>&nbsp;[[Brierley JD, Gospodarowicz MK, Wittekind C, editors. TNM classification of malignant tumours. 8th ed. Oxford (UK): Wiley-Blackwell; 2017.]]) and the FIGO staging system.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-07-15T11:53:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 471,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 384,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63623,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 228,
        "headingId": 384,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The behaviour of these tumours is dictated by the highest-grade component {31318970}. Therefore, these tumours are graded as high-grade carcinoma irrespective of the relative percentages of serous or clear cell carcinoma present.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T16:53:21"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]